# scripts/copy_lift.private.baseline — the ceiling for EXACT private copies of shared decisions.
# scripts/copy_lift.py --gate holds it. MEASURED at 99476a5, not chosen:
#
#   python3 scripts/copy_lift.py --private-copies
#
# An EXACT private copy is a function in an EMITTER (`lower.al`, `aarch64.al`, `riscv64.al`,
# `wat.al`) whose normalized signature AND body equal a function in shared code, matched by SHAPE
# and not by the `FAMILIES` name list. Name-matching is what `FAMILIES` already does, and it is
# why criterion 2 was unenforced: pasting the shared span scan into `lower.al` under a fresh name
# left `--gate` at rc=0 with zero mentions of it (measured on this tree before this file existed).
# A census that only knows the copies someone already named does not hold "the private copies are
# gone".
#
# WHY AN ADDITION RULE WITH A BASELINE RATHER THAN A REFUSAL OF ALL NINETEEN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All 19 exist on main today, so refusing exact copies outright would turn the gate red before any
# new defect was added — which is not evidence, that is a broken instrument. The repo's own rule
# (AGENTS.md "Strict forms") is the same one every other check here follows: refuse only a NEW
# occurrence against the merge base, and let an existing one be acknowledged where it cannot move
# yet. This file is that merge-base snapshot, by NAME so a removal is auditable: a bare count would
# let one copy leave and another arrive unseen.
#
# WHY NONE OF THE 19 IS FORCED BY LAYERING — checked, not assumed
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The obvious defence of a copy in `lower.al` is that the shared original sits ABOVE it and the
# emitter may not call upward. It already does, so the defence fails: `lower.al` references
# `driver::` (2 sites), `cli::` (1), `sema::` (5), `parser::` (3), `lower_layout::` (81),
# `lower_ctx::` (1), `ast::` (25), `ir::` (6). Every module holding one of these 19 originals is
# already imported by the file holding the copy, so each copy is one call away from being deleted.
# The graph is already bidirectional (`driver::` calls `lower::` 62 times, `sema::` 43,
# `lower_layout::` 22), so lifting adds no edge that does not already exist.
#
# Each line is `name<TAB>emitter:line<TAB>original`. The line numbers are where the COPY stands
# today and move with any edit; the NAME is what the ratchet holds. Removing a copy means deleting
# its line here in the same PR, and the count falling is coverage gained — say so in the PR body
# and let `--private-copies` print the proof.
private=19
streq	lower.al:290	driver.al:62
colon_pos	lower.al:437	driver.al:1245
is_builtin_int_name	lower.al:787	cli.al:3490
_lnhash	lower.al:1948	lower_layout.al:410
gref_ident_byte	lower.al:2109	sema.al:19617
vis_show_span	lower.al:2229	lower_layout.al:353
vis_show_str	lower.al:2232	lower_layout.al:356
is_dyn_type	lower.al:9494	driver.al:2009
decl_at	lower.al:13328	driver.al:52
decl_get	lower.al:13330	lower_layout.al:127
tuple_first_start	lower.al:15577	lower_layout.al:1841
expr_is_int_lit	lower.al:16313	sema.al:13528
is_int_scalar_type	lower.al:17015	sema.al:5716
is_float_scalar_type	lower.al:17023	sema.al:5721
comptime_kind_of_name	lower.al:24822	sema.al:17489
guard_expr_is_num	lower.al:25014	parser.al:2789
guard_cmp	lower.al:25023	sema.al:17280
guard_stmt_ret_expr	lower.al:25039	sema.al:17641
